A||||2||SteveJ||160 degree thermostats||||1||
Z||000000||SteveJ||06-25-2001||12:17 PM||||Can someone please tell me if there is a legitimate use for these things. I see lots of people putting them in their cars here on the forum but I don't understand why. Maybe there is something I am missing. Do you need this to keep the temp down when using NOX ?<P>My grandfather used to tell me they were outdated. Said they were for the "old days" when they used alcohol and water in the radiator.<P>Modern systems are COLD at that temperature. Is this the old, "if a little is good a lot is better" philosophy but now applied to engine cooling?||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||06-25-2001||12:33 PM||||IMO They hurt more than they help unless your setup has 'special' needs.  FACT - Engines are designed to have the heads run about 200 to 210 so working backwords from that, automotive engineers use 195 thermostats.  Here's the clincher... Not just GM but most if not all of them use a 195 degree thermostat for optimum engine performance.  Now if you have a high compression engine (11.5/1 or higher) or the wind blows into it at 20 lbs you may have special needs (detonation) and want to try to lower head temps by lowerering coolant temps.  I wonder what a new porcshe themostat opens at???<P>edited for typos<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||06-25-2001||03:54 PM||||air cooled cars dont have a thermostat!!!<P>I have 3.1 turbo, with 195, car wont run without spitting, backfire, surging, everything in the book. 180 made it a little better, 160 cured everything. Now I have a screamer and temp stays in the bottom third even with ac on and 95* in traffic. And it does still put out enough heat in the middle of Ohio winter too.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
